+# Hardware tests: the capture session
+
+One session, about 30 minutes at the Rane One. It records what the One sends (the real protocol, spec §6); everything
+later in the build depends on it.
+
+**Nothing is sent to the controller during the session.** Only `tools/capture` runs, and it opens the MIDI port
+read-only. `~/.rane-armed` is not needed (it is only for sending).
+
+Run everything from the repo checkout, on the run branch `harness/rane-one-mixxx-2026-10-19`.
+
+## Before you start
+
+About 5 minutes.
+
+- Tape a mark on each platter's edge and one on the chassis next to it.
+- Motor off on both decks; both platters standing still.
+- Known positions (the session asks you to write them down in its setup note):
+  - channel faders down, crossfader centre
+  - EQ, gain and filter knobs at 12 o'clock
+  - pitch faders at centre (the detent), 33 selected
+  - note where the Stop Time knob and the rear torque switch are
+- Source selectors on USB.
+- Mixxx, Serato and anything else that uses the One closed (the port is exclusive; `tools/capture` exits 5 if it is held).
+- The One plugged in and powered on, except for step 1, which asks you to power it off first.
+- The checkout on branch `harness/rane-one-mixxx-2026-10-19`: `git branch --show-current`.
+
+## Run
+
+1. Dry run, no hardware involved. It shows the 7 groups, 24.75 min, and the optional extras:
+
+   ```
+   tools/capture-session --list
+   ```
+
+2. A 5 s check with the One on and Mixxx closed. Wiggle one knob while it runs. The summary on stderr should show
+   messages; `cc` lines are in the file. Exit 5 means another program holds the port: close it. Exit 4 means the
+   One is not listed (`amidi -l`).
+
+   ```
+   tools/capture --port 'Rane ONE MIDI 1' --seconds 5 --out /tmp/check.jsonl
+   ```
+
+3. The full session:
+
+   ```
+   tools/capture-session --label census
+   ```
+
+   It writes `captures/<date>-census/` (one `<nn>-<step>.jsonl` per step, plus a `README.md`). Keys, each followed
+   by Enter:
+
+   | When | Key |
+   |------|-----|
+   | at a step | Enter starts the recording; `s` skips the step (it asks for a note) |
+   | while recording | Enter stops it (timed steps stop by themselves, with a bell) |
+   | after a recording | Enter keeps the take; `r` records it again; then a one-line note (an empty take needs `k`) |
+   | after a failure | Enter tries again; `s` skips; `k` keeps what it recorded as a partial take |
+   | Ctrl-C | stops; a recording in progress is kept unreviewed and `--resume` offers it for review |
+
+   If the tool stops part-way (Ctrl-C, a crash, the terminal closing), continue where it left off:
+
+   ```
+   tools/capture-session --label census --resume
+   ```
+
+   For the other options: `tools/capture-session --help`.
+
+### Fallback if `tools/capture` itself fails
+
+Use ALSA's recorder, then convert. Find the sequencer client first (the number changes on replug; always go by name):
+
+```
+arecordmidi -l
+mkdir -p captures/<date>-census
+arecordmidi -p 'Rane ONE' -b 60 -t 1000 captures/<date>-census/<nn>-<step>.mid
+```
+
+Stop it with Ctrl-C after the step, then:
+
+```
+tools/capture-convert captures/<date>-census/<nn>-<step>.mid
+```
+
+(`tools/capture-convert --help` for `--out`, `--force`.) Use the step names from `tools/capture-session --list`, and
+write the README line for each step by hand. `arecordmidi` also only reads.
+
+## Time needed
+
+From `tools/capture-session --list`:
+
+| Group | Recordings | Minutes |
+|-------|-----------:|--------:|
+| §6.1 Power-on dump | 1 | 1.00 |
+| §6.2 Census | 5 | 10.30 |
+| §6.3 Platter, motor off, by hand | 8 | 3.75 |
+| §6.4 Platter, motor on | 8 | 6.75 |
+| §6.5 Pitch fader with the motor on | 2 | 1.20 |
+| §6.6 Shift+Slip and the motor | 1 | 1.00 |
+| §6.7 Strip search / needle drop | 1 | 0.75 |
+| **Total (26 recordings)** | | **24.75** |
+
+Plus about 5 minutes of setup that is not a step, so under 30 minutes in all. After §6.7 the tool offers 8 optional
+extras (4.40 min: Shift pads in modes 2–8, and deck 2's fast/alt turns, hold, backspin and both stops). Press `s` on
+each to skip them; skipping them loses nothing the build needs.
+
+## Commit
+
+The capture is about 100 MB of JSONL, roughly 12 MB once git packs it. Check what you have, then commit:
+
+```
+ls captures/<date>-census | wc -l
+git add captures/<date>-census
+git commit -m "captures: <date>-census (capture session, <n> steps, <skipped> skipped)"
+git push -u origin harness/rane-one-mixxx-2026-10-19
+```
+
+`<n>` is the number of `.jsonl` files kept, `<skipped>` the number of steps you skipped (the README lists them). Any
+leftover `*.jsonl.part` file is an unreviewed take: resume and keep or redo it, or delete it, before adding.
+
+To undo before committing: delete `captures/<date>-census/`. Nothing else changes.
+
+## Report back
+
+In `.harness/runs/rane-one-mixxx-2026-10-19/INBOX.md`, on the run branch, under `## Answers`, write:
+
+- the session directory (`captures/<date>-census`)
+- which steps were redone or skipped, and why
+- the things only eyes can tell:
+  - did the platter speed follow the pitch fader (§6.5)?
+  - what did Shift+Slip and the rear torque switch do (§6.6)?
+  - is there a strip search or needle drop (§6.7)?
+  - did the ten turns by hand end on the mark (§6.3)?
+- anything odd: a step that felt wrong, a bell that didn't ring, a control that sent nothing
