+# M2 plan · Capture and replay (2026-10-19)
+
+Goal (spec §5 M2): recording the controller is cheap, labelled and repeatable, and a recording can be played back
+into Mixxx with its timing. This plan settles what M2-02..M2-08 build: the capture file format, the SMF library,
+how `tools/capture` reads the One (D14), replay through the `tools/midi-send` gate, the census CSV, the guided
+session Michael runs, and the outline of `HARDWARE-TESTS.md`. Build-only policy: every M2 test runs on fixtures,
+FIFOs and stubs. The live steps (the M2-04 Mixxx replay, Michael's session) are written out under Replay and
+Capture session.
+
+Machine facts were read on Michael's desktop on 2026-10-20 08:23–08:31 with read-only commands (Evidence below).
+Nothing was sent to any MIDI port. MIDI facts cite `captures/2026-10-19-quick/`. Everything else about the
+protocol stays UNVERIFIED until the session's captures exist.
+
+Conventions, as in M1 and M3: tools are executables in `tools/`. The new ones are Node 24 ES modules without an
+extension (`#!/usr/bin/env node`). `package.json` has `"type": "module"`, and Node 24.21 runs an extensionless
+file as ESM (checked, Evidence). Each tool prints `usage: tools/<name> …` and exits 2 on bad arguments, with
+cases added to `tests/tools-usage.test.mjs`. Exit 1 means bad input or I/O failure. Zero dependencies. Tests write
+only under a temp dir. Shared code lives in `tools/lib/`: `midi-parse.mjs` (M1), `jsonl.mjs` and `smf.mjs` (new).
+M2-08 checks that no tool keeps a private copy.
+
+## JSONL format
+
+Every capture is a `.jsonl` file: UTF-8, one JSON object per line, each line ending in `\n`, with no header line
+and no blank lines. Each object has **exactly these seven keys, in this order**:
+
+| Key | Type | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `t_us` | integer ≥ 0 | Host microseconds since capture start. For `tools/capture`, the start is the moment the device open returned. For `tools/capture-convert`, it is SMF tick 0. Non-decreasing through the file. Messages that arrived in one `read()` share a value. |
+| `bytes` | string | The message's raw bytes as uppercase hex, no spaces, status byte included (running status resolved), e.g. `B0065C`. SysEx carries `F0 … F7`. A truncated SysEx has no `F7`. **This is the ground truth.** The other keys are derived from it. |
+| `type` | string | `on` `off` `pat` `cc` `pc` `cp` `pb` (channel messages, the same words as `describe()` in `midi-parse.mjs`), `sysex`, `mtc` `spp` `song` `tune` (system common), `clock` `start` `continue` `stop` `sense` `reset` (real-time). |
+| `ch` | 1..16 or `null` | Channel, 1-based (status low nibble + 1). `null` for system messages. |
+| `d1` | 0..127 or `null` | First data byte (note, CC number, program, pressure, LSB). `null` when the message has none. |
+| `d2` | 0..127 or `null` | Second data byte (velocity, CC value, MSB). `null` when the message has none. |
+| `value` | integer or `null` | The message's value: `d2` for `on` `off` `pat` `cc`; `d1` for `pc` `cp` `mtc` `song`; **14-bit `d1 \| d2 << 7` (0..16383, centre 8192) for `pb`** and `spp`; `null` otherwise. |
+
+A note-on with velocity 0 stays `type: "on"`, `value: 0`. Captures show what the device sent. Readers that want
+"release" treat `off` and `on` with value 0 alike. Three lines as `tools/capture-convert` will write them from
+`captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.mid`. They are the file's first three channel events, at ticks 2651, 2651 and
+2673 of a 1 ms tick (decoded by hand from the hex dump in Evidence):
+
+```jsonl
+{"t_us":2651000,"bytes":"B0065C","type":"cc","ch":1,"d1":6,"d2":92,"value":92}
+{"t_us":2651000,"bytes":"E0153D","type":"pb","ch":1,"d1":21,"d2":61,"value":7829}
+{"t_us":2673000,"bytes":"B0065D","type":"cc","ch":1,"d1":6,"d2":93,"value":93}
+```
+
+(Pitch bend 7829 = 8192 − 363, the `-363` that aseqdump printed in `platter.txt`.) A SysEx line looks like
+`{"t_us":10,"bytes":"F07E7F0601F7","type":"sysex","ch":null,"d1":null,"d2":null,"value":null}`. That one is
+synthetic: no SysEx from the One has been seen.
+
+`tools/lib/jsonl.mjs` holds the only implementation of this format:
+
+```js
+export function toRecord(msg, t_us)        // parser message {status, data, truncated?} → record (the 7 keys)
+export function formatLine(rec)            // record → line, keys in table order, ends in "\n"
+export function parseLine(line, where)     // line → record; throws Error("<where>: …") on any violation
+export function readJsonl(path)            // → records; errors name "path:line"
+export function createJsonlWriter(path, { force } = {}) // opens path ("wx", or "w" with force) → { write(msgs, t_us), close(), count }; writeSync per call
+```
+
+`parseLine` is strict. It rejects a missing key, an extra key, a non-integer or negative `t_us`, lowercase or
+odd-length hex, and any derived key that disagrees with re-deriving it from `bytes` through `midi-parse.mjs`.
+`readJsonl` also rejects a `t_us` that goes backwards. Keys are strict, so fixtures document themselves in a
+sibling `README.md`, never in an extra key. That settles M2-05 step 1: `tests/fixtures/README.md` says which
+`.jsonl` files are synthetic.
+
+Size: the quick capture's 58,650 messages make 4.8 MB of JSONL (≈ 82 bytes per line), and gzip takes that to
+0.6 MB (measured with a scratch conversion, Evidence). Motor-on, the One sent ≈ 2000 messages/s (below). So a
+60 s motor-on step is ≈ 9–10 MB, and a whole session ≈ 100 MB in the working tree and ≈ 12 MB packed. Files are
+committed as plain `.jsonl`: each stays far under GitHub's 50 MB warning, and git compresses them.
+
+## SMF library
+
+`tools/lib/smf.mjs`, a pure ES module (the two file wrappers are its only I/O). It is used by
+`tools/capture-convert` (arecordmidi's files in) and `tools/replay` (files for aplaymidi out).
+
+```js
+export function readSmf(buffer)            // → { format, division, tracks: [[event]], events: [event] }
+export function writeSmf({ division, tempo, events }) // format 0, one track → Buffer
+export function ticksToMicros(smf)         // → function(tick) → µs, from division and the tempo map
+export function readSmfFile(path); export function writeSmfFile(path, opts) // tmp + rename
+// event: { tick, kind: "midi"|"sysex"|"meta", bytes: Uint8Array, meta?: { type, data }, escape?: true }
+```
+
+- **Read:** `MThd` with length ≥ 6 (extra header bytes skipped), formats 0 and 1, then each `MTrk`. Unknown chunk
+  types are skipped. Format 2 and SMPTE division (high bit set) are rejected with an error naming the offset;
+  arecordmidi writes neither unless given `-f`. Delta times are variable-length quantities of up to 4 bytes (more
+  is an error). Running status is resolved inside the track. Meta (`FF type len data`) and SysEx (`F0 len data`,
+  `F7 len data` escapes) cancel running status, as the SMF 1.0 spec says. A SysEx event's `bytes` is `F0` plus
+  its data, so the `F7` is included when the file has one. `events` is all tracks merged by tick, stable within
+  a tick (track order, then file order). End of track ends the track. A track that ends without it and runs out
+  of bytes is an error.
+- **Tempo:** `FF 51 03 tt tt tt` sets µs per quarter note from its tick on (default 500000 before the first
+  tempo event). `ticksToMicros` integrates the tempo map, so µs(tick) = Σ over segments of Δticks × tempo /
+  division, in exact integer arithmetic (BigInt) and rounded to the nearest µs at the end. With division 1000 and
+  tempo 1000000, one tick is 1000 µs.
+- **Write:** always format 0, one track. Tick 0 holds `FF 51 03` with the given tempo. Then come the events and
+  `FF 2F 00`. A channel message (status 0x80–0xEF) is written with its status byte every time (no running status).
+  A complete SysEx (`F0 … F7`) is written as `F0 <VLQ len> <data…F7>`. Anything else is an error, naming the
+  event: system common, real-time and a truncated SysEx. In an SMF, `FF` is a meta event, and F1–FE would need
+  `F7` escapes that aplaymidi may not honour. Events must be in non-decreasing tick order (an error otherwise).
+  Default `division` 1000 and `tempo` 1000000.
+- Tests (`tests/smf.test.mjs`, M2-02 step 1) cover: a hand-made format-0 file (header, tempo, time signature, CC,
+  pitch bend, a note pair with running status, a SysEx, end of track) that round-trips with identical events and
+  ticks; VLQs of exactly 1, 2 and 3 bytes (0x7F, 0x80/0x3FFF, 0x4000/0x1FFFFF), both read and written; division
+  1000 + tempo 1000000 → 1000 µs per tick, plus a mid-file tempo change; and errors for a truncated track and for
+  SMPTE division.
+- `tools/capture-convert <file.mid> [--out file.jsonl] [--force] [--allow-dropped]` (M2-02): `readSmf`, then for
+  each `midi` or `sysex` event (meta skipped), one per-file `createParser().feed(bytes)`. A `midi` event or a
+  complete `F0 … F7` must end in its own message (only a SysEx left open before it, now truncated, may precede
+  it). An F0 packet without F7 and an F7 event (`escape: true`) carry raw bytes and may yield any number of
+  messages (D16: SysEx divided across events, SMF 1.0). At the end, `flush()` writes a still-open SysEx truncated.
+  Each message becomes `toRecord(msg, µs(tick))`, written next to the input with `.mid`/`.midi` replaced by
+  `.jsonl`. Dropped bytes fail the conversion unless `--allow-dropped`. It refuses to overwrite unless `--force`,
+  and it writes through a temp file, so a failure keeps the old output. For `platter.mid` this gives 58,650 lines,
+  29,323 of them `cc` with `d1` 6, and the last `t_us` 48,390,000 (last tick 48,390, Evidence). Every message
+  matches aseqdump's `platter.txt` in order (M2-02, 2026-10-20).
+
+## Capture backend
+
+**D14 *default*** (`docs/decisions.md`; feature M2-01 step 3 calls it D10, but D10 already holds the launcher's
+start check, so this decision is D14). `tools/capture` reads the rawmidi device read-only and stamps each read
+with `process.hrtime`. arecordmidi plus `tools/capture-convert` is the manual fallback.
+
+```
+tools/capture (--port <name> | --device <path>) --out <file.jsonl> [--seconds <n>] [--wait <s>] [--force]
+```
+
+- **By name:** `--port 'Rane ONE MIDI 1'` runs `amidi -l` and takes the row whose name column (everything after
+  `Dir Device`) equals the argument exactly. Its `hw:C,D,S` becomes `/dev/snd/midiC<C>D<D>`. Card numbers change
+  on replug (CLAUDE.md), so the tool resolves the name every time and never stores a number. An absent name, or one
+  listed only as an output, exits **4** with `capture: no MIDI input port named '<name>' (amidi -l lists: …)`
+  (built: the row's `Dir` must contain `I`; two input rows with the name exit 1, "use --device"; a row on a
+  subdevice other than 0 exits 1, because `/dev/snd/midiC<C>D<D>` opens the first free subdevice and can't choose).
+  `CAPTURE_SND_DIR` replaces `/dev/snd` for the tests, which point it at a temp dir holding a FIFO. With `--wait <s>`, `amidi -l` is polled
+  every 100 ms for up to `s` seconds before giving up. The power-on step needs this, because the One's port
+  doesn't exist while it is off. Once the name appears, the open is retried for up to 2 s on `ENOENT`/`EACCES`,
+  since udev may not have set the node's ACL yet.
+- **`--device <path>`** skips the lookup. It is for tests (a FIFO) and the fallback of last resort.
+- **Read-only, and why the read runs in a child process.** Opening the device and reading it happen in a child
+  that is the same file run as `tools/capture --reader <path>`. That child is the only code that opens the device,
+  and it does so exactly as `fs.openSync(path, "r")`: `O_RDONLY`, so the kernel can't take a write through this
+  fd. It loops on `fs.readSync(fd, buf)` (blocking). Right after each read returns it takes
+  `process.hrtime.bigint()` and writes a frame to fd 1 with `fs.writeSync(1, frame)`. `process.stdout.write`
+  must not be used: the child's event loop never runs, so it would never flush. A frame is 1 byte of kind,
+  8 bytes of ns, 4 bytes of length, then the payload. Built with four kinds: `0` attempt (sent before each open
+  try, so the parent's busy timer runs from the latest try, not across the ENOENT retries), `1` opened (length 0,
+  right after the open returns; its stamp is `t_us` 0), `2` data (the bytes of one read) and `3` error (JSON
+  `{phase, code, message}`, then the child exits 1). A `readSync` that returns 0 bytes is EOF: a FIFO
+  whose writer closed, never a live rawmidi device. The child then exits 0, and the parent finishes as if it had
+  been stopped (exit 0). The parent runs the
+  event loop: it parses frames, feeds `midi-parse.mjs`, and `writeSync`s one JSONL line per message, so a kill
+  loses nothing already read. The output is opened only by `createJsonlWriter` in `tools/lib/jsonl.mjs`, so
+  `tools/capture`'s only `openSync` is the device's `"r"`. It also handles Ctrl-C, `--seconds` and the open
+  timeout. To stop, it sends the
+  child SIGTERM, drains the pipe to EOF, prints a summary to stderr (messages per type, `dropped`, duration) and
+  exits 0.
+  Why a child: a worker thread blocked in `readSync` on the rawmidi device made `process.exit()` hang (2 min 25 s,
+  until SIGTERM). A SIGTERM to a separate process ends both a blocked `read()` and a blocked `open()` (checked on
+  a FIFO, Evidence). Both processes use the monotonic clock, so stamps agree. hrtime is CLOCK_MONOTONIC, and NTP
+  steps don't move it.
+- **Busy device.** A rawmidi input substream on the One can't be opened twice. A blocking open **waits
+  indefinitely** while another program holds it (seen while `aseqdump -p 'Rane ONE'` was subscribed). The other
+  way round, aseqdump got `Cannot connect from port 24:0 - Resource temporarily unavailable` while our open held
+  it (Evidence). So when the device is a character device and the child has not reported "opened" within 2 s,
+  the parent kills it and exits **5**: `capture: <port> is busy: another program has it open (Mixxx, aseqdump,
+  arecordmidi, Serato's bridge?); close it and retry`. A FIFO has no timeout, because its open waits for the
+  writer by design. Close Mixxx before a capture session. If Mixxx is bound to the One, it holds the port, and a
+  running capture stops Mixxx from binding it too.
+- **Ends:** SIGINT (Ctrl-C; in a terminal the child gets it too, which is expected) or SIGTERM to the parent,
+  both a clean stop with exit 0; `--seconds n` after the open; or the device going away (`ENODEV`/`EIO`: lines
+  written so far are kept, `capture: device gone after <t> s`, exit 1). A partial message at the end counts in `dropped`. `--out` must not exist unless
+  `--force`. The file is created (through `createJsonlWriter`) before the device is opened, so a bad path fails
+  first. If the device never opens (busy, open error, a stop first), the still-empty file is removed again, so a
+  redo needs no `--force`; a file that existed before (`--force`) is left in place, emptied, never deleted. A stop
+  before the open exits 1 (`stopped before the device opened`), not 0: no capture exists. A failing output write
+  (ENOSPC) stops the reader and exits 1 naming the lines written. SIGHUP is a stop like SIGTERM, and an `exit`
+  handler kills the reader on any other parent exit, so it never stays behind holding the device; only a SIGKILL
+  of the parent can orphan it (it then dies on its next read, EPIPE). Known limit: a stop signal landing in the
+  microseconds between the reader's `readSync` returning and its frame write loses that one read; a frame cut
+  short is reported (`lost a partial frame`). The busy exit (5) can't be reached with a FIFO, so it is not in the
+  suite; it was checked live (Evidence, M2-03).
+- **Timestamps:** one stamp per `read()`. snd-usb-audio delivers what arrived in each USB transfer, so the
+  messages of one transfer share a `t_us`. A message split across two reads takes the stamp of the read that
+  completes it. The platter's own timing is expected to come from the controller's
+  counter (spec §2, UNVERIFIED), so host µs order the messages and measure gaps. They do not time the platter.
+- Tests (`tests/capture.test.mjs`, M2-03) use `--device <fifo>`: three chunks, one of which splits a CC and one
+  holds a SysEx, give one line per message with `t_us` non-decreasing and `bytes`/`type`/`value` per the table.
+  `--seconds 1` ends by itself in under 3 s. A stubbed `amidi -l` (with the real 2026-10-20 listing `IO  hw:2,0,0
+  Rane ONE MIDI 1`) resolves to `/dev/snd/midiC2D0`. An absent name exits 4. The source check (M2-03 step 3) is
+  `grep -nE "openSync|createWriteStream|open\\(" tools/capture`. It must show exactly one line, the device's
+  `fs.openSync(path, "r")`. `grep -nE "aplaymidi|amidi -[^l]|amidi\"" tools/capture` must show nothing.
+- **Fallback (manual, when `tools/capture` misbehaves):**
+  `arecordmidi -p 'Rane ONE' -b 60 -t 1000 <step>.mid`, Ctrl-C to stop, then `tools/capture-convert <step>.mid`.
+  `-b 60 -t 1000` gives division 1000 and tempo 1000000, which is the header of `captures/2026-10-19-quick/
+  platter.mid` (`MThd … 0000 0001 03e8`, `FF 51 03 0F 42 40`). One tick is 1 ms, so this path's `t_us` is coarse
+  to 1 ms. aseqdump accepted the client name `Rane ONE`, and arecordmidi uses the same ALSA address parser.
+  The fallback reads through the sequencer and never writes either.
+
+## Replay
+
+```
+tools/replay (--to-port <name> | --dry-run [--out <file.mid> [--force]] [--to-port <name>]) [--from <s>] [--to <s>] [--speed <k>] <file.jsonl>
+```
+
+JSONL → SMF (format 0, division 1000, tempo 1000000 µs, so 1 tick = 1 ms, the same as arecordmidi's files above) →
+`tools/midi-send play <client>:<port> <file.mid>`. `tools/replay` **never calls aplaymidi itself**. The gate stays
+the only sender (CLAUDE.md), so its refusal rules apply to replays too. `tools/midi-send` keeps no log, so
+replay prints the exact `tools/midi-send play …` line it runs to stderr. A session that replays copies that line
+into its progress entry (CLAUDE.md: log what you sent).
+
+- **Selection and timing:** keep records with `from·10⁶ ≤ t_us < to·10⁶` (defaults 0 and ∞). The event time is
+  `(t_us − from·10⁶) / speed`, and the tick is `round(time / 1000)`. Ticks are rounded from absolute times, never
+  summed from rounded deltas, so error never builds up: every event is within 0.5 ms of its scaled `t_us`.
+  `--speed 2` halves every gap. Order within a tick is kept. Events are taken from `bytes` (via `midi-parse.mjs`,
+  so a hand-edited file can't smuggle in a non-message), never rebuilt from the derived keys. **Only channel
+  messages are replayed.** SysEx, system common and real-time records are dropped and counted on stderr
+  (`replay: dropped 3 sysex, 0 system, 12 real-time`). The SMF writer can't carry most of them, and CLAUDE.md
+  never sends SysEx. With no `--from`,
+  leading silence is kept, so the replay starts exactly as the capture did.
+- **Port by name:** `--to-port 'Midi Through'` reads `aconnect -o` and takes the client whose quoted name equals
+  the argument exactly (`client 14: 'Midi Through' [type=kernel]` → `14:0`). An absent name exits 4.
+- **Never to the One:** a name matching `/rane/i` exits 3 (`replay: refusing to replay into the Rane One`), and so
+  does a destination from which a Rane client is reachable. That uses **the gate's own transitive walk**:
+  `Connecting To` subscriptions, plus every port of a user client it reaches. Replay runs it **whatever the arm
+  state**, because midi-send runs it only when disarmed. The awk walk moves out of `tools/midi-send` into
+  `tools/lib/rane-route.awk`, used by both, with no change in behaviour (midi-send's safety tests must stay green,
+  and a replay test adds a Midi Through → user client → Rane route). Why: D13 allows sends to the One, and
+  the dev profile may bind Mixxx to both ports. A capture replayed through Mixxx to the One would be outbound bytes
+  no one has traced (D13: replays go through Midi Through). If Mixxx is bound to the One as well, replay is
+  refused until that binding is removed.
+- **The SMF** goes to a `mkdtemp` directory and is deleted after `midi-send` returns. `--dry-run --out` writes it
+  and sends nothing. `--dry-run` prints the event count, duration and the `midi-send` command it would run.
+  `--from`/`--to` that keep nothing exit 1. Exit codes from `midi-send` (3, 4) pass through.
+- **As built (M2-04):** `--dry-run --to-port` runs the name lookup and route walk and sends nothing. Two clients
+  with the same name exit 4 (no guessing). `--out` is refused if it exists, unless `--force`, so a slip can't
+  overwrite the capture. The route is checked once, before sending, as in midi-send. A stop (SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP)
+  before midi-send runs removes the temp directory; during the replay it is passed to midi-send's player. Either
+  way replay exits 128+n. midi-send's failures other than 3 and 4 become 1, so exit 2 always means replay's own
+  usage. The walk is `tools/lib/rane-route.awk` (byte-identical to the old inline program), found through
+  `readlink -f` with `CDPATH` cleared.
+- **Precision:** aplaymidi schedules on the ALSA sequencer queue with 1 ms ticks here, and Mixxx polls MIDI every
+  5 ms on Linux (spec §2). Replay timing is therefore good to ~1 ms. It is fit for driving the mapping, not for
+  re-measuring the host timing of the original capture.
+- Tests (`tests/replay.test.mjs`, M2-04): `--dry-run --out` on a fixture, read back with `readSmf`, gives the same
+  bytes, and every tick·1000 is within 1000 µs of the scaled `t_us`. `--speed 2`, and `--from 20 --to 37` on a
+  fixture that spans 0–60 s. Stubbed `aconnect`/`amidi`/`aplaymidi` on PATH (as in `tests/midi-send.test.mjs`)
+  check that `--to-port 'Midi Through'` calls `tools/midi-send play 14:0 <tmp>.mid`, and the stubs' call log plus
+  a source grep show no `aplaymidi` in `tools/replay`. A Rane name exits 3 with no call.
+- **Live step (M2-04 step 3, dev Mixxx only, `WAYLAND_DISPLAY` set; otherwise skipped with a note):**
+  1. `tools/null-sink up && tools/install-mapping && tools/mixxx-dev start` (Mixxx on Midi Through only).
+  2. Dry run: `tools/replay --dry-run --out /tmp/harness/…/r.mid --from 19 --to 21
+     captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.jsonl`. Expect **3396 events, 1697 of them CC 6**. The motor starts at
+     tick 19,236 (the note-on), so second 19 holds only 1400 events and second 20 holds 1996 (scratch walk and
+     reviewer recount, Evidence). `--from 20 --to 22` would give 3992 events and 1996 CC 6.
+  3. `tools/replay --to-port 'Midi Through' --from 19 --to 21 captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.jsonl`, then
+     `grep -c` the CC 6 input lines in `dev-profile/mixxx.log`. Expect 1697, which is "about 2000" in M2-04
