+## Census
+
+```
+tools/census <file.jsonl>… [--out reference/rane-one-mk1-midi-map.csv] [--shift <ch>:<note>] [--comment <text>]
+tools/census --diff reference/rane-one-mk2-djay-midi-map.csv [<file.jsonl>…]
+```
+
+One row per control-shaped message group. The CSV header row is exactly
+
+```
+control,status,channel,number,min,max,sends_on_release,pair_14bit_msb,pair_14bit_lsb,shift,source_file
+```
+
+and the columns mean:
+
+| Column | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `control` | Label. Auto-made as `<kind> ch<ch> <number>` (`cc ch1 6`, `note ch1 0`, `pb ch1`) until M6 names controls. |
+| `status` | The kind's status nibble in hex without a channel: `90` note (on and off both land here), `A0` poly pressure, `B0` CC, `C0` program, `D0` channel pressure, `E0` pitch bend. |
+| `channel` | 1..16. |
+| `number` | Note or CC number (the MSB number for a 14-bit pair). Empty for `C0`, `D0`, `E0`. |
+| `min`, `max` | Over `value`. For notes, only note-ons with velocity > 0 count. For a 14-bit pair it is the combined `msb << 7 \| lsb`. For pitch bend, 0..16383. |
+| `sends_on_release` | `true`/`false`. For a note: a release (`off`, or `on` with value 0) was seen for it. For a CC: its values take exactly two settings, 0 and one other, and they alternate (a CC-style button). Otherwise `false`. |
+| `pair_14bit_msb`, `pair_14bit_lsb` | The two CC numbers (e.g. `9` and `41`). Filled when CC n (0–31) and CC n+32 on the same channel come as adjacent pairs (≥ 90 % of the rarer one's messages sit directly next to one of the other on that channel). The pair is then one row, and neither CC gets a row of its own. |
+| `shift` | With `--shift <ch>:<note>`: `true` if every message of the row came while that note was held, `false` if none did, `both` otherwise. Empty without `--shift`. The MK2 map has Shift on channel 15 (0-based), note 50, i.e. `--shift 16:50`. That is UNVERIFIED for the MK1 until the census shows it. |
+| `source_file` | Repo-relative capture file(s) the row's messages came from, `;`-joined and sorted. |
+
+- Rows are sorted by `channel`, then `number` (empty first), then `status`. SysEx and system messages get no row.
+  They are counted on stderr (SysEx with its distinct first 4 payload bytes), so a power-on dump in SysEx form is
+  not lost silently.
+- The output starts with `#` comment lines (`# rane-one-mk1-midi-map · tools/census <args> · <date>`, then
+  `--comment` text), then the header row. Readers of this CSV skip leading `#` lines.
+- On `captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.jsonl` (M2-05 step 4) the census gives exactly three rows: `cc ch1 6`,
+  `pb ch1`, `note ch1 0` (aseqdump shows note 0 ch 0 on/off pairs at lines 14593/14660 and 58117/58336 of
+  `platter.txt`). It is saved with `--comment "partial: one quick platter capture; the full census comes from the
+  capture session"`.
+- **`--diff`** reads the MK2 CSV (`section,djay_keyPath,channel,data,msgType,extra`). Its `channel` is 0-based:
+  the values run 0..15 (counted 2026-10-20). `msgType` 1 is a note (Shift; `turntable1.pitchBendPlus` ch 0 data
+  11) and 3 is a CC (`mixer.crossfade` ch 15 data 8). The 6 rows of type 0 (ch 14, data 0–5, djay's "Unknowns")
+  have an unknown kind. MK2 rows with the same channel/data/msgType collapse to one, with keypaths `|`-joined.
+  A row matches when channel (MK2 + 1) and number agree. The output is CSV on stdout:
+  `diff,status,channel,number,mk1_control,mk2_section,mk2_keypaths,same_kind`, where `diff` is `only-mk1`,
+  `only-mk2` or `both` and `same_kind` is `true`/`false`/`unknown`, sorted like the census. The MK2 map is a hint
+  (D6). The diff shows where to look, and it never moves an MK2 fact into the MK1 file.
+- Tests (`tests/census.test.mjs`, M2-05) use the synthetic `tests/fixtures/census-small.jsonl`, documented in
+  `tests/fixtures/README.md`. It holds a note on/off pair, a note with no release, a CC swept 0..127, a 14-bit pair
+  CC 9/41 and a pitch bend, on two channels. Only the pair is `sends_on_release` `true`. CC 9/41 is one row. The
+  sweep is 0/127. The order is checked. Then a `--diff` run against a 3-row MK2 extract checks one row of each
+  `diff` kind.
+
+**As built (M2-05, 2026-10-21).** The rules live in `tools/lib/census.mjs` (pure); `tools/census` does the files.
+Refinements of the rules above:
+- 14-bit pair: "next to" means the neighbouring message on that channel (a note in between breaks it). When both
+  CCs have the same count, *each* must meet the 90 % share. Each accepted pair is reported on stderr with its
+  counts and share, so a false pair (say, a button on CC n+32 pressed during a CC n sweep) can be seen. Values
+  follow the stream's order: MSB first unless LSB→MSB neighbours outnumber MSB→LSB ones. Then each LSB completes a
+  value with the last MSB (LSB-only updates count), and a half that comes before any of the other completes nothing
+  (a capture that opens mid-pair). A CC that is a pair in one file and plain in another gives two rows (7-bit and
+  14-bit values are never mixed).
+- A note seen only as releases has an empty `min`/`max`. `sends_on_release` is always `false` for a pair, `A0`,
+  `C0`, `D0` and `E0`.
+- A CC button's alternation is judged within each file (a file may start on either setting).
+- `--shift` judges each message by the Shift state before it (so the Shift note's own row is `both`). Shift counts
+  as up at the start of each file.
+- `source_file` is repo-relative; a file outside the repo keeps its absolute path.
+- `--out` refuses an existing file unless `--force`. `--diff` prints to stdout and takes only capture files. A
+  `--comment` with a line break, and the same capture given twice, are usage errors.
+- `--diff` with no capture file takes the MK1 side from `reference/rane-one-mk1-midi-map.csv` (M2-05 step 3 runs it
+  that way); it gives the same output as diffing the capture the file came from.
+- `--diff`: a pair row also matches an MK2 row at its LSB number. Each (MK1 row, MK2 group) match is one `both`
+  line, so a row can appear twice. `status` is the MK1 kind for `both` and `only-mk1`, the MK2 kind for `only-mk2`
+  (empty for type 0). Against the real map (595 rows, 195 groups) the quick capture gives `both` 2, `only-mk1` 1
+  (`pb ch1`), `only-mk2` 193. The 2 `both` lines have `same_kind` `false`: MK2 channel 0 data 0 is a CC
+  (`libraryRotary`) and data 6 a note (`loadTrack`), while the One MK1 sent note 0 and CC 6 there.
+
+## Capture session
+
+`tools/capture-session [--label <label>] [--resume] [--list] [--port <name> | --device <path> | --capture-cmd
+<cmd>]` walks Michael through spec §6 one recording at a time. It creates `captures/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<label>/`
+(default label `census`, local date). It refuses an existing directory unless `--resume`, which continues at the
+first step that has no README line. Each step records through `tools/capture` (or `--capture-cmd`, a test stub
+taking the same arguments) into `<nn>-<step>.jsonl`. `nn` runs 01..32 in the order performed, so `ls` lists the
+files in session order, and the §6 group number appears in `--list` and in README.md.
+
+`tools/capture` runs as a child with stdin `'ignore'`, so the session owns the keyboard. Enter stops a recording
+by sending the child SIGINT, which is capture's clean stop. A Ctrl-C at the session's own terminal also reaches
+the child (same process group). The session catches it, lets the current capture finish cleanly, keeps that file
+as an unreviewed take, writes the README and exits 130. `--resume` then offers that step again.
+
+Per step: print the group, the §6 text and the prompt. `Enter` starts the recording, `s` skips it. While it
+records, the tool shows the elapsed time, with a bell (`\a`) and `time's up` at the expected length on timed
+steps, and `Enter` stops (timed steps also stop by themselves). Then `Enter` keeps the take, `r` re-records it
+(the file is overwritten), and a one-line note prompt follows. README.md gets one line per step, written when
+it is kept or skipped (a redo replaces the line, never adds one):
+
+```
+- `07-hand-d1-slow.jsonl` · §6.3 Platter by hand, deck 1 · 1532 messages, 31.2 s · note: <Michael's note>
+- `29-strip-d1.jsonl` · §6.7 Strip search · skipped · note: no strip on the MK1?
+```
+
+The README starts with the date, the tool's `git rev-parse --short HEAD`, the port as resolved
+(`Rane ONE MIDI 1 = hw:2,0,0`) and a setup note typed at the start (Stop Time and torque switch positions). A
+capture that fails with exit 4 or 5 prints what to fix (power, a USB cable, close Mixxx) and offers the step again.
+Nothing is ever sent: the session only runs `tools/capture`, and `~/.rane-armed` is not read.
+
+**Before starting** (spec §6, verbatim): "tape a mark on each platter's edge and one on the chassis next to it;
+motor off; all faders and knobs at a known position (write it down in the prompt)." The opening prompt writes that
+known position down: channel faders and crossfader down/centre, EQ, gain and filter knobs at 12 o'clock, pitch
+faders at centre (the centre detent), 33 selected, Stop Time and torque as Michael normally keeps them (he types
+them into the setup note), both platters still, source selectors on USB.
+
+The §6 steps, with each recording's prompt, file and expected duration. Durations include reading the prompt.
+Timed steps stop by themselves. **As built (M2-06, D17):** the numbers and the 28.15 min total below are the plan's.
+The tool numbers the recordings 01–34, with 8 of them as optional extras at the end. See "As built" after the
+totals; `tools/capture-session --list` is the source of truth.
+
+**§6.1 Power-on dump:** "power the One off, start recording, power it on, wait 10 s."
+
+| nn · file | Prompt | Expected |
+|---|---|---|
+| `01-power-on` | Power the One OFF now (rear power switch). When it is off, press Enter, then power it ON as soon as the screen says `RECORDING: power on now`. Recording stops by itself 10 s after its MIDI port appears. | 1.0 min |
+
+How it runs: after Enter, if `Rane ONE MIDI 1` is absent from `amidi -l`, the tool starts `tools/capture --wait
+60 --seconds 10` at once and prints `RECORDING: power on now`. `--seconds` counts from the open, so the
+recording runs 10 s after the port appears. If the port is still present, it prints `still there: is it off? Wait
+for RECORDING before powering on`, polls up to 30 s for it to vanish, and then does the same. If it never
+vanishes, the One is in a standby that keeps USB alive. The tool records 10 s from then, writes that into the
+README line, and asks for a power cycle while recording (a re-enumeration ends that capture with exit 1, which
+the step reports and offers to redo). The burst on first open (CLAUDE.md) is most likely buffered until the host
+polls the device, so opening a moment after enumeration should still catch it (UNVERIFIED; this step checks it).
